WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.100
A defunct falcon 9 stage impact on the moon gives astronomers a calibrated data point on crater

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:14.580
formation from a known mass and velocity. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 55. A falcon 9 second

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:20.600
stage is on course to strike the lunar surface on Wednesday, creating a new crater whose formation

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:27.240
astronomers will observe directly. The stage follows a trajectory set after its primary mission,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:34.020
with impact timing fixed by orbital mechanics. Reports indicate the hardware mass and velocity

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:40.740
are known quantities, allowing precise modeling of the resulting excavation. Ground-based telescopes

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:46.880
will capture the event to refine impact physics models. The stage originates from a prior

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.540
falcon 9 flight whose upper stage was left in a disposal orbit after payload deployment.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.540
The same falcon 9 upper stage will deliver a known kinetic energy to the lunar regolith,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.420
enabling direct comparison between predicted and observed crater dimensions.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:10.200
Observers note the impact site lies in a region free of prior human hardware, simplifying

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.260
baseline measurements. Data collected will test scaling laws for hypervelocity impacts in vacuum.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:22.400
Remember, we covered Starship developments yesterday. Today's flight moves that forward because

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:29.260
the booster descent validated recovery hardware ahead of future attempts. Starship flight 13 completed

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:34.740
its primary test objectives with the super heavy booster executing a controlled descent profile.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:41.400
The ship upper stage achieved orbital insertion and performed reentry maneuvers before loss of

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:47.420
signal. Post-flight data review highlighted successful heat shield performance on the ship during

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:53.420
peak heating. The flight advanced the campaign toward rapid reuse goals. Additional coverage

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:59.180
noted the booster's descent as a key step in validating recovery hardware ahead of future tower

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:05.760
catch attempts. State officials are advancing permitting for a coastal southwest Louisiana launch

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:12.040
complex intended for falcon and Starship vehicles. The site selection targets proximity

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:17.820
to existing Gulf infrastructure while adding geographic redundancy to Texas operations.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.180
Environmental reviews and local support are cited as near completion.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:29.700
Construction timelines remain tied to federal approvals. A related Reddit thread notes

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:34.860
ongoing state-level steps and compares the proposal to existing Texas facilities.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:41.740
The vessel Normand Ranger is reported stabilizing ship 40 at sea following its recovery.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:48.400
Current speed of three knots places the toe approximately 12 days from dampier at the observed

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:55.720
distance of 852 nautical miles. The restricted maneuverability status confirms attachment to

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:01.020
the ship upper stage. Operations continue to prepare the vehicle for final transport.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:07.120
The update specifies that the vessel's speed remains insufficient to fully overcome local

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:13.520
currents during the open ocean transit. Multiple Falcon 9 missions are listed for Cape Canaveral

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:20.020
pads through the remainder of August. The manifest includes both commercial and starlink payloads

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:26.340
with no announced delays. Pad turnaround intervals remain consistent with prior high cadence periods.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:32.860
The schedule reflects ongoing booster reuse across the fleet. The report lists specific

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:38.080
windows for each mission and notes that the cadence aligns with the current operational tempo

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.360
at the Florida site. Investors have submitted questions ahead of the first post-IPO earnings,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.940
including requests for a pink painted rocket and a starship named Shippy McShip Face.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:56.500
The queries focus on starship timelines, starlink economics, and AI infrastructure spend.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:02.660
Organizers compiled the list from public submissions. Community members are coordinating travel

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:08.780
from South Padre Island to starbase for the next starship flight test, noting limited shuttle

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:15.220
options and exploring boat services. Plans include side trips to Houston for Johnson Space Center

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:20.480
visits. Participants emphasize the potential for a booster catch on the upcoming attempt.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:29.180
Worth keeping an eye on, SpaceX reported Q2 revenue of $6.87 billion, alongside a widened

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:36.380
net loss of $4.2 billion in its first earnings as a public company. The results reflect continued

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.900
investment across starship development and starlink constellation growth.

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:47.500
Analysts note the loss expansion occurs against a backdrop of thawing lock-up shares valued

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:55.100
at $12.3 billion. Resolution depends on demonstrating narrower losses in subsequent quarters as

00:04:55.100 --> 00:05:00.920
reuse rates improve. The earnings release coincides with the first public company quarterly

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:09.120
filing since the June 2026 IPO. On the AI front, Musk stated that Grock can generate a complete

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.620
historical film comparable to the movie Odyssey, pointing to expanded generative capabilities in

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:21.460
video and narrative synthesis. The claim ties directly to Grock's integration path through

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:27.040
developer tools and platform distribution. The statement appears in coverage of recent model

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:32.060
demonstrations and highlights potential downstream uses in content creation workflows.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:40.220
XAI plans to remove 69 unpermitted turbines from the Tennessee Mississippi Colossus campus by July

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:47.160
2027, following congressional record requests. The extension allows continued operation while

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:53.320
compliance documentation is prepared. Separate reporting notes that the turbines remain in place

00:05:53.320 --> 00:06:00.100
until the 2027 deadline amid ongoing demands for operational records. From an engineering

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:05.540
standpoint, the Falcon 9 second stage impact on the moon supplies a controlled experiment

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:12.140
in hypervelocity collision mechanics. The stage carries a documented dry mass and arrives at a

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:18.600
velocity set by its residual orbital energy after mission completion. Because both parameters are

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:23.760
known, the kinetic energy delivered to the regolith can be calculated directly from conservation

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:30.200
of momentum without relying on estimates. This removes one layer of uncertainty that normally

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:36.560
affects natural impactor studies. The resulting crater will therefore serve as a calibration point

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:43.060
for scaling laws that relate projectile mass, velocity, and target properties to final morphology.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.400
Observers can compare the measured diameter and depth against predictions derived

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:54.180
from laboratory and simulation data. Any deviation will highlight the influence of lunar

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.620
regolith, porosity, or layering that current models treat only approximately.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:04.260
The single known impact also constrains the contribution of angle of incidence,

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.440
which is fixed by the stage's approach trajectory. Future missions can use the same

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:15.660
approach to benchmark hardware disposal or intentional impact experiments. The data will tighten

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:21.020
the relationship between delivered energy and ejected volume, improving models used for both

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:28.860
planetary defense and in C2 resource assessments. The calibration value extends beyond crater morphology

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:35.240
to thermal and seismic signatures recorded during the event. Ground-based instruments can correlate

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:41.820
the known impact energy with observed flash intensity and any detectable seismic waves propagating

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:47.580
through the lunar crust. These correlations refine remote sensing techniques applied to other

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:53.980
solar system bodies where impactor parameters remain unknown. Over time, the single data point

00:07:53.980 --> 00:08:00.040
reduces systematic error in population estimates of near-earth objects and in assessments of

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:06.690
regolith gardening rates. Engineers planning future lunar landers or resource extraction hardware

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:12.230
can incorporate the refined scaling relationships to predict excavation volumes more accurately.

